## Recovering platform tooling after EKS cluster loss

Example scenario:

In analytical-platform-production / 312423030077 the EKS cluster production-dBSvju9Y has disappeared. This has meant that there is no Control Panel or associated tooling for users.

### Assumptions

No other infrastructure is affected and there are no problems in provisioning infrastructure.

### Steps to take

1. Open Loop – find the incident process and follow that
2. *Investigate EKS restore*
3. Contact AWS with a priority support request – can the infra be restored even though it appears destroyed?
4. If restoration is not possible, re-run Terraform apply from [prod tooling repo](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform/tree/main/terraform/aws/analytical-platform-production/cluster) to rebuild the cluster
5. Bootstrap Helm Flux from the CLI ([documentation](https://silver-dollop-30c6a355.pages.github.io/))
   1. Alternatively, take the opportunity to remove CLI operations and recreate as Terraform
6. Note Control Panel should be operational at this point (as other Control Panel recovery is out of scope for this exercise)
7. Create a new tooling release
8. Namespace initialisation – this is done when a user authenticates, or can be done in bulk in Control Panel (bootstrap user chart)
9. Run the EFS restore to recover user home directories and data. This may take some time, but users should be able to interact with the platform at this point while restoration is underway.

## Recovering the Control Panel after database and deployment loss

Example scenario:

Pingdom alerts, or users reporting 5xx errors when accessing Control Panel. Investigations reveal that the Control Panel deployment has vanished, including the database. The EKS cluster is operational.

### Assumptions

No other infrastructure is affected and there are no problems in provisioning infrastructure.

### Steps to take

1. Open Loop – find incident process and follow that
2. Restore RDS snapshot in Terraform via `snapshot_identifier` in Terraform
3. Test database connectivity and that data is populated
4. Update AWS Secrets Manager with new DB host info –
   `production/control-panel-db-password`
   1. The secret will be synced in around 5 minutes
5. Control Panel is deployed via Flux, so if this is operational then Control Panel will be redeployed. If this is not the case, refer to the **Tooling and home directories recovery** runbook. Check the `#analytical-platform-flux` channel.
6. Minor task – check the user feedback S3 bucket (the location Control Panel saves user form data)
7. If necessary, commence Redis restoration via Terraform.
   Control Panel will be operational without this, but Redis is required for tooling. Redis host is specified in the Flux repo. (Flux will then reconcile this.)
8. Celery workers – also deployed via Flux / Helm chart, but check
9. Continue monitoring for other 5xx Sentry errors in `#analytical-platform-high-priority-notifications`
